# Class: obo:HP_0001251 (Ataxia)

AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref <https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0736-9199>) obo:IAO_0000115 obo:HP_0001251 "Ataxia refers to impaired coordination of voluntary muscle movement. Cerebellar ataxia refers to ataxia due to dysfunction of the cerebellum. This causes a variety of elementary neurological deficits including asynergy (lack of coordination between muscles, limbs and joints), dysmetria (lack of ability to judge distances that can lead to under- or overshoot in grasping movements), and dysdiadochokinesia (inability to perform rapid movements requiring antagonizing muscle groups to be switched on and off repeatedly).")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasAlternativeId obo:HP_0001251 "HP:0001253")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasAlternativeId obo:HP_0001251 "HP:0002513")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasAlternativeId obo:HP_0001251 "HP:0007050")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasAlternativeId obo:HP_0001251 "HP:0007157")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasDbXref obo:HP_0001251 "SNOMEDCT_US:85102008")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasDbXref obo:HP_0001251 "UMLS:C0007758")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym obo:HP_0001251 "Cerebellar ataxia")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:id obo:HP_0001251 "HP:0001251")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:comment obo:HP_0001251 "Ataxia can be differentiated into dyssynergy, asynergy, dysmetria (hypometria, hypermetria), dysdiadochokinesis, gait ataxia, truncal ataxia, limb ataxia, and dysarthria). Note: This term does not include sensory ataxia.")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label obo:HP_0001251 "Ataxia")
SubClassOf(obo:HP_0001251 obo:HP_0011442)

# Class: obo:HP_0001252 (Hypotonia)

AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref <https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0736-9199>) Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref "PMID:21418198") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:HP_0001252 "Hypotonia is an abnormally low muscle tone (the amount of tension or resistance to movement in a muscle). Even when relaxed, muscles have a continuous and passive partial contraction which provides some resistance to passive stretching. Hypotonia thus manifests as diminished resistance to passive stretching. Hypotonia is not the same as muscle weakness, although the two conditions can co-exist.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasAlternativeId obo:HP_0001252 "HP:0011398")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasDbXref obo:HP_0001252 "SNOMEDCT_US:398151007")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasDbXref obo:HP_0001252 "SNOMEDCT_US:398152000")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasDbXref obo:HP_0001252 "UMLS:C0026827")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasSynonymType hp:layperson) oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym obo:HP_0001252 "Low muscle tone")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref <https://orcid.org/0000-0002-6548-5200>) Annotation(oboInOwl:hasSynonymType hp:layperson) oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym obo:HP_0001252 "Low or weak muscle tone")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym obo:HP_0001252 "Muscle hypotonia")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym obo:HP_0001252 "Muscular hypotonia")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasNarrowSynonym obo:HP_0001252 "Central hypotonia")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasNarrowSynonym obo:HP_0001252 "Peripheral hypotonia")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:id obo:HP_0001252 "HP:0001252")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:comment obo:HP_0001252 "Hypotonia can be caused by abnormalities of the central nervous system, any element of the motor unit (including the lower motoneuron), or both. Hypotonia is not a specific diagnosis, but can be observed in hundreds of genetic and other diseases. The first distinction to make when assessing a child with hypotonia is whether decreased muscle tone is a result of an abnormality of the central nervous system (CNS), peripheral neuromuscular system, or a combined abnormality involving both. Clinical findings suggestive of an abnormality of the CNS may include hyperreflexia, cognitive developmental delay, and seizures. In contrast, physical findings pointing towards a neuromuscular origin may include weakness, lack of antigravity movements, muscle atrophy, fasciculations, and/or diminished reflexes, most often in the context of normal cognitive function. The HPO term does not distinguish between these etiologies. Additional HPO terms should be used as required to describe associated features.")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label obo:HP_0001252 "Hypotonia")
SubClassOf(obo:HP_0001252 obo:HP_0003808)

# Class: obo:HP_0001254 (Lethargy)

AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000115 obo:HP_0001254 "A state of fatigue, either physical or mental slowness and sluggishness, with difficulties in initiating or performing simple tasks. Distinguished from apathy which implies indifference and a lack of desire or interest in the task. A person with lethargy may have the desire, but not the energy to engage in personal or socially relevant tasks.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasDbXref obo:HP_0001254 "SNOMEDCT_US:214264003")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasDbXref obo:HP_0001254 "UMLS:C0023380")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym obo:HP_0001254 "Dullness")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym obo:HP_0001254 "Inaction")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym obo:HP_0001254 "Inactivity")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym obo:HP_0001254 "Languor")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasSynonymType hp:layperson) oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym obo:HP_0001254 "Lethargy")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym obo:HP_0001254 "Slowness")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym obo:HP_0001254 "Torpor")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:id obo:HP_0001254 "HP:0001254")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:comment obo:HP_0001254 "Apathy and lethargy may co-occur.")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label obo:HP_0001254 "Lethargy")
SubClassOf(obo:HP_0001254 obo:HP_0011446)

# Class: obo:HP_0001257 (Spasticity)

AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref <https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0736-9199>) obo:IAO_0000115 obo:HP_0001257 "A motor disorder characterized by a velocity-dependent increase in tonic stretch reflexes with increased muscle tone, exaggerated (hyperexcitable) tendon reflexes.")
