# Class: obo:HP_0001382 (Joint hypermobility)

AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref <https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0736-9199>) Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref "PMID:28145606") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:HP_0001382 "The capability that a joint (or a group of joints) has to move, passively and/or actively, beyond normal limits along physiological axes.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasAlternativeId obo:HP_0001382 "HP:0001378")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasAlternativeId obo:HP_0001382 "HP:0001380")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasAlternativeId obo:HP_0001382 "HP:0001383")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasAlternativeId obo:HP_0001382 "HP:0001388")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasAlternativeId obo:HP_0001382 "HP:0002771")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasAlternativeId obo:HP_0001382 "HP:0005034")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasAlternativeId obo:HP_0001382 "HP:0005692")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasDbXref obo:HP_0001382 "SNOMEDCT_US:298181000")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasDbXref obo:HP_0001382 "UMLS:C1844820")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasSynonymType hp:layperson) oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym obo:HP_0001382 "Double-Jointed")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym obo:HP_0001382 "Extensible joints")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasSynonymType hp:layperson) oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym obo:HP_0001382 "Flexible joints")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym obo:HP_0001382 "Hyperextensible joints")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym obo:HP_0001382 "Increased joint mobility")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasSynonymType hp:layperson) oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym obo:HP_0001382 "Increased mobility of joints")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym obo:HP_0001382 "Joint hyperextensibility")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym obo:HP_0001382 "Joint hyperflexibility")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym obo:HP_0001382 "Joint hyperlaxity")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym obo:HP_0001382 "Joint laxity")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasSynonymType hp:layperson) oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym obo:HP_0001382 "Joints move beyond expected range of motion")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasSynonymType hp:layperson) oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym obo:HP_0001382 "Lax joints")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasSynonymType hp:layperson) oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym obo:HP_0001382 "Loose-jointedness")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym obo:HP_0001382 "Joint instability")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym obo:HP_0001382 "Joint ligamentous laxity")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym obo:HP_0001382 "Ligamentous laxity")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:id obo:HP_0001382 "HP:0001382")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:comment obo:HP_0001382 "Synonyms of joint hypermobility (JH) include joint laxity and double-jointedness. In general terms, joint hyperlaxity is often considered a further synonym of JH. JH may be caused by a number of factors including intraarticular disease and injury or slacking of extraarticular structures such as joint capsules, ligaments, and muscles. Establishing whether a joint is hypermobile or not is a relatively easy task and it is carried out by (i) using professional tools, such as the orthopedic goniometer; (ii) following specific procedures; and (iii) comparing the measured range of motion (ROM) with normal parameters. When JH is observed at one or a few types of joints (usually fewer than five) it may be defined as localized joint hypermobility (LJH). Typically, LJH affects a single small or large joint and may be bilateral (e.g., bilateral genu recurvatum due to knee hyperextensibility). LJH may be inherited, but it may be an acquired trait related to, for example, past trauma, joint disease, surgery, or training (e.g., spine hypermobility). In individuals with JH at multiple sites (usually five or more), the term generalized joint hypermobility (GJH) is preferred.")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label obo:HP_0001382 "Joint hypermobility")
SubClassOf(obo:HP_0001382 obo:HP_0011729)

