# Class: <https://www.genenames.org/data/genegroup/#!/group/3429> (Hexokinase family)

AnnotationAssertion(terms:description <https://www.genenames.org/data/genegroup/#!/group/3429> "Hexokinases phosphorylate a variety of hexose sugars, although their preferred substrate is glucose. GCK has a single hexokinase domain while the other family members have two hexokinase domains but only HK2 has been shown to have two active sites.")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasSynonymType obo:OMO_0003000) oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym <https://www.genenames.org/data/genegroup/#!/group/3429> "HK")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label <https://www.genenames.org/data/genegroup/#!/group/3429> "Hexokinase family")
SubClassOf(<https://www.genenames.org/data/genegroup/#!/group/3429> obo:SO_0005855)

# Class: <https://www.genenames.org/data/genegroup/#!/group/3463> (NALCN channel complex subunits)

AnnotationAssertion(terms:description <https://www.genenames.org/data/genegroup/#!/group/3463> "NALCN regulates the resting membrane potential by mediating the Na+ leak current in neurons, and it functions as a channelosome in complex with NALF1, UNC79, and UNC80. Dysfunction of the NALCN channelosome causes a broad range of neurological and developmental diseases called NALCN channelopathies in humans.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym <https://www.genenames.org/data/genegroup/#!/group/3463> "NALCN channelosome")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym <https://www.genenames.org/data/genegroup/#!/group/3463> "NALCN-FAM155A-UNC79-UNC80 channel complex")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label <https://www.genenames.org/data/genegroup/#!/group/3463> "NALCN channel complex subunits")
SubClassOf(<https://www.genenames.org/data/genegroup/#!/group/3463> obo:SO_0005855)

# Class: <https://www.genenames.org/data/genegroup/#!/group/3469> (Ribonucleotide reductase subunits)

AnnotationAssertion(terms:description <https://www.genenames.org/data/genegroup/#!/group/3469> "The importance of ribonucleotide reductase (RNR) lies in its unique ability to catalyze the rate-limiting step of the reduction of ribonucleotides (NDPs) to the corresponding deoxyribonucleotides (dNDPs), the precursors of DNA. Mammalian RNRs consist of two homodimeric subunits, which associate to form the holoenzyme. The large RRM1 dimer contains the catalytic site, whereas the smaller dimer (RRM2 or RRM2B) contains an oxygen-requiring di-iron tyrosyl-radical site, which is essential for catalysis.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym <https://www.genenames.org/data/genegroup/#!/group/3469> "RNRs")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasSynonymType obo:OMO_0003000) oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym <https://www.genenames.org/data/genegroup/#!/group/3469> "RRM")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym <https://www.genenames.org/data/genegroup/#!/group/3469> "Ribonucleoside-diphosphate reductases")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label <https://www.genenames.org/data/genegroup/#!/group/3469> "Ribonucleotide reductase subunits")
SubClassOf(<https://www.genenames.org/data/genegroup/#!/group/3469> obo:SO_0005855)

# Class: <https://www.genenames.org/data/genegroup/#!/group/3489> (DMPK kinase family)

AnnotationAssertion(terms:description <https://www.genenames.org/data/genegroup/#!/group/3489> "The DMPK kinases are members of the AGC kinase family with a conserved domain architecture of N-terminal kinase domains followed by a predicted coiled-coil domain and C-terminal regulatory domains that mediate a variety of protein-protein and protein-membrane interactions. While many AGC kinases are regulated by phosphorylation of motifs in their activation loop or C-terminal tail, the DMPK kinases are not. Instead, their N-terminal capped-helix bundle (CHB) domain cooperates with the C-terminal tail to maintain the kinase domain in a homodimeric, active conformation with an ordered activation loop. Protein kinases of the DMPK family are critical regulators of actomyosin contractility in cells.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym <https://www.genenames.org/data/genegroup/#!/group/3489> "DMPK family")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym <https://www.genenames.org/data/genegroup/#!/group/3489> "dystonia myotonica protein kinase family")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label <https://www.genenames.org/data/genegroup/#!/group/3489> "DMPK kinase family")
SubClassOf(<https://www.genenames.org/data/genegroup/#!/group/3489> obo:SO_0005855)

# Class: <https://www.genenames.org/data/genegroup/#!/group/3496> (L-lactate dehydrogenase family)

AnnotationAssertion(terms:description <https://www.genenames.org/data/genegroup/#!/group/3496> "L-lactate dehydrogenase (LDH, EC1.1.1.27) is a key enzyme that catalyzes the interconversion of L-lactate and pyruvate with nicotinamide adenine dinucleotide (NAD+) as a coenzyme. This enzyme exists in a tetrameric form by combination of M (muscle, LDHA) and H (heart, LDHB) subunits in various proportions in different somatic tissues. There are five major tetrameric LDH complexes consisting of LDHA and/or LDHB subunits. Expression of LDHC, LDHAL6A and the intronless gene LDHAL6B is restricted to testes where they may play a role in spermatogenesis.")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label <https://www.genenames.org/data/genegroup/#!/group/3496> "L-lactate dehydrogenase family")
SubClassOf(<https://www.genenames.org/data/genegroup/#!/group/3496> obo:SO_0005855)

# Class: <https://www.genenames.org/data/genegroup/#!/group/3503> (2-arachidonoylglycerol hydrolases)

AnnotationAssertion(terms:description <https://www.genenames.org/data/genegroup/#!/group/3503> "The endocannabinoid 2-arachidonoylglycerol (2-AG) is a lipid mediator involved in various physiological processes. In response to neural activity, 2-AG is synthesized post-synaptically, then activates pre-synaptic type 1 cannabinoid recep  tors, resulting in transient and long-lasting reduction of neurotransmitter release. The signalling competence of 2-AG is tightly regulated by the balanced action between 'on demand' biosynthesis and degradation. The major enzymatic route for 2-AG inactivation in brain is via hydrolysis; three serine hydrolases (MGLL, BHD6 AND ABHD12) account for approx. 99% of brain 2-AG hydrolase activity.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym <https://www.genenames.org/data/genegroup/#!/group/3503> "2AG hydrolases")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label <https://www.genenames.org/data/genegroup/#!/group/3503> "2-arachidonoylglycerol hydrolases")
SubClassOf(<https://www.genenames.org/data/genegroup/#!/group/3503> obo:SO_0005855)

# Class: <https://www.genenames.org/data/genegroup/#!/group/3507> (MCU complex subunits)

AnnotationAssertion(terms:description <https://www.genenames.org/data/genegroup/#!/group/3507> "The mitochondrial calcium uniporter channel exists as a channel composed of a total of 4 MCU subunits and up to 4 SMDT1 subunits. In some cases, the dominant-negative MCU paralog, MCUB, replaces MCU subunits within the complex. The channel is gated by dimers of MICU1/2 or, in some tissues, dimers of MICU1/3. The accessory subunit MCUR1 also binds to and regulates the channel. Two uniporter channels can dimerize, and the function of these dimers is regulated by interactions between the MICU2 subunit associated with each channel.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym <https://www.genenames.org/data/genegroup/#!/group/3507> "MCUC")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym <https://www.genenames.org/data/genegroup/#!/group/3507> "mitochondrial calcium uniporter channel")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym <https://www.genenames.org/data/genegroup/#!/group/3507> "mitochondrial calcium uniporter complex")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label <https://www.genenames.org/data/genegroup/#!/group/3507> "MCU complex subunits")
SubClassOf(<https://www.genenames.org/data/genegroup/#!/group/3507> obo:SO_0005855)

