# Class: <https://www.genenames.org/data/genegroup/#!/group/3567> (Dynamin superfamily)

AnnotationAssertion(terms:description <https://www.genenames.org/data/genegroup/#!/group/3567> "The dynamin superfamily comprises an assortment of multi-domain GTPases, found from bacteria to man, that are distinguished from typical GTPases of the Ras, Rab and G-protein families by their modular structure, relatively large size (>70 kDa), and low affinity for guanine nucleotides. In addition, they display a conserved propensity to self-assemble into polymeric arrays, the dynamics of which are regulated by an autonomous, assembly-stimulated GTPase activity.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym <https://www.genenames.org/data/genegroup/#!/group/3567> "Dynamin and dynamin-like GTPases")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label <https://www.genenames.org/data/genegroup/#!/group/3567> "Dynamin superfamily")
SubClassOf(<https://www.genenames.org/data/genegroup/#!/group/3567> <https://www.genenames.org/data/genegroup/#!/group/3565>)

# Class: <https://www.genenames.org/data/genegroup/#!/group/3569> (Dynamins)

AnnotationAssertion(terms:description <https://www.genenames.org/data/genegroup/#!/group/3569> "Based on domain architecture, the dynamin superfamily is broadly divided into two groups, classical dynamins and dynamin-related proteins. The common features shared by all dynamins and Drps are the presence of a large N-terminal GTPase domain, a middle domain (MD) and a GTPase effector domain (GED). While GTPase domain binds and hydrolyses GTP, MD and GED play important roles in oligomerization and regulation of GTPase activity. Classical dynamins contains two additional domains namely, pleckstrin homology domain (PHD) involved in membrane binding and a proline-rich domain (PRD) containing several SH3-binding sites important for protein–protein interactions.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym <https://www.genenames.org/data/genegroup/#!/group/3569> "Classical dynamins")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasSynonymType obo:OMO_0003000) oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym <https://www.genenames.org/data/genegroup/#!/group/3569> "DNM")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym <https://www.genenames.org/data/genegroup/#!/group/3569> "Dynamin family")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label <https://www.genenames.org/data/genegroup/#!/group/3569> "Dynamins")
SubClassOf(<https://www.genenames.org/data/genegroup/#!/group/3569> <https://www.genenames.org/data/genegroup/#!/group/3567>)

# Class: <https://www.genenames.org/data/genegroup/#!/group/3571> (Translational GTPases)

AnnotationAssertion(terms:description <https://www.genenames.org/data/genegroup/#!/group/3571> "Translational GTPases (trGTPases) play key roles in facilitating protein synthesis on the ribosome. Despite the high degree of evolutionary conservation in the sequences of their GTP-binding domains, the rates of GTP hydrolysis and nucleotide exchange vary broadly between different trGTPases.")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym <https://www.genenames.org/data/genegroup/#!/group/3571> "protein-synthesizing GTPases")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym <https://www.genenames.org/data/genegroup/#!/group/3571> "trGTPases")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label <https://www.genenames.org/data/genegroup/#!/group/3571> "Translational GTPases")
SubClassOf(<https://www.genenames.org/data/genegroup/#!/group/3571> <https://www.genenames.org/data/genegroup/#!/group/3565>)

# Class: <https://www.genenames.org/data/genegroup/#!/group/3600> (Pyrroline-5-carboxylate reductase family)

AnnotationAssertion(terms:description <https://www.genenames.org/data/genegroup/#!/group/3600> "PYCR1, PYCR2, and PYCR3 are members of the pyrroline-5-carboxylate reductase family of enzymes. They catalyze the final step of proline synthesis by converting pyrroline-5-carboxylate (P5C) into proline (EC 1.5.1.2). PYCR1 and PYCR2 localize to mitochondria, where their P5C substrate is generated from glutamate by P5C Synthase (P5CS), whereas PYCR3 is found in the cytosol and its P5C substrate is generated from ornithine by ornithine aminotransferase (OAT). PYCR1 and PYCR2 are both associated with human diseases, but with very different clinical manifestations. PYCR1 mutations cause Cutis Laxa (CL, MIM #612940 and 614438). In contrast, PYCR2 mutations cause hypomyelinating leukodystrophy type 10 (HLD10, MIM #616420). There are no diseases yet associated with PYCR3.")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasSynonymType obo:OMO_0003000) oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym <https://www.genenames.org/data/genegroup/#!/group/3600> "PYCR")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym <https://www.genenames.org/data/genegroup/#!/group/3600> "Pyrroline-5-carboxylate reductases")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label <https://www.genenames.org/data/genegroup/#!/group/3600> "Pyrroline-5-carboxylate reductase family")
SubClassOf(<https://www.genenames.org/data/genegroup/#!/group/3600> obo:SO_0005855)

# Class: <https://www.genenames.org/data/genegroup/#!/group/3607> (Elastases)

AnnotationAssertion(terms:description <https://www.genenames.org/data/genegroup/#!/group/3607> "Elastases are proteases capable of degrading mature cross-linked elastin. Elastases are found in different classes of protease: the matrix metalloproteinases (MMPs), aspartic proteases, serine proteases and cysteine proteases. Elastin cleavage by certain enzymes requires pre-damage of elastin, occurring as a result of aging processes, as has for instance been described for the serine protease human leukocyte elastase (ELANE) or metalloendopeptidase neprilysin (MME).")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label <https://www.genenames.org/data/genegroup/#!/group/3607> "Elastases")
SubClassOf(<https://www.genenames.org/data/genegroup/#!/group/3607> obo:SO_0005855)

